## Keep your whole body warm

Attacks are often triggered when your **core body temperature** drops, so it helps to keep all of you warm, not just your hands and feet.

- wear **layers**, a hat and a scarf in cold weather
- wear **gloves** and **warm socks**, and keep your feet dry
- try **hand warmers** or heated gloves, socks or insoles
- keep your home warm, and warm your car up before a journey
- use **oven gloves** or gloves when taking food from the freezer or fridge
- watch out for sudden changes in temperature, such as air conditioning or cold supermarket aisles

## During an attack

- move somewhere **warmer** if you can
- warm your hands or feet **gently**, for example in your armpits or in **warm** (not hot) water
- **wriggle** your fingers and toes, or swing your arms in big circles, to get the blood flowing
- do not use very hot water or put your hands right next to a heater, as numb skin can burn easily

## Manage stress

Stress and anxiety can trigger attacks. Breathing exercises, yoga, walking or other ways to relax may help. Regular **exercise** also improves your circulation and helps you stay warm.

## Other things that may help

- **stop smoking**
- some people find **caffeine** makes symptoms worse, so try cutting down on tea, coffee and cola
- look after your skin, using moisturiser to stop it cracking
- check your fingers and toes for **sores** that are not healing, and get them checked quickly

## Help with heating costs

Keeping warm can be expensive. You may be able to get help with your energy bills. Read about [money off bills](/support/money-off-bills/) and our guide to [disability and surviving winter](/articles/disability-and-surviving-winter/).

## Support

**Scleroderma and Raynaud's UK (SRUK)** is a charity with information, a helpline and support for people with Raynaud's and scleroderma.
